AB  - Multi-agent pursuit-evasion games (MPEG) are a cornerstone of multi-agent systems, requiring the pursuer team to capture the evader team through effective collaboration. Although multi-agent reinforcement learning (MARL) has achieved promising results in general multi-agent settings, its ability to support effective collaboration in MPEG scenarios remains limited, especially in dynamic and uncertain environments, where insufficient adaptability and coordination often result in suboptimal performance against the evader strategies. In this paper, we propose a multi-agent mutual-advising model-based reinforcement learning (MA2MB) framework, which addresses these challenges by incorporating three key components: (1) an evasion model that extends adversary modeling by explicitly estimating strategy-level uncertainty of evaders, (2) a mutual-advising world model that improves team coordination by enabling structured inter-agent policy advising during imagination-based planning, and (3) a collaborative adversarial multi-agent soft actor–critic algorithm for simulating adversarial scenarios and optimizing the pursuer strategies. Experimental results show that, compared with the baseline MARL approaches in three MPEG environments, the pursuer team in our MA2MB method demonstrates state-of-the-art performance, highlighting the critical role of mutual-advising in MPEG scenarios. © 2026 Elsevier B.V.
