AB  - Toxic language online is a major challenge to a polite communication, particularly in a setting where codeswitching and many languages are practiced. The moderation tools that are based on machine learning are no longer able to operate offline; they need a substantial amount of computing power, big datasets, and a continuous connection to the internet. In this work, a lightweight, rule-based, and multilingual awareness system of harmful language identification and re-framing will be presented, aiming to unlock the real-time use of the system on devices with minimal resources. The actual novelty in this case is its offline capability, scalability, and anti-censorship. Such combination clears the way to its exploitation in a very diverse range of environments, including not only classrooms and discussion groups but also gaming worlds. The engine is based on a well put together list of offensive words, along with their more polite equivalents. It supports more than fifteen languages, such as English, Hindi, Tamil and is also sensitive to codemixed input. The dataset of 5,000 sentences was used to compare the performance of the model, and the performance achieved was 96.2% on a dataset of 5,000 sentences, which corresponds to the $\mathbf{F 1}$ score of 0.94. With a reaction time of less than $\mathbf{1 0 0}$ milliseconds, less than $\mathbf{2 0}$ megabytes storage space, and positive user reviews, this system will be an effective and viable solution to sustaining a healthy online communication.
