AB  - Large Language Models (LLMs) agents with ability to predict the further world states could avoid security risks. A core capability of the world model is the precise control over the transition of world states based on action instructions. However, existing world models aim to construct a general model, the complexity and countless rules of these worlds make it challenging to determine whether the generated world states accurately reflect the actions. To accurately investigate and evaluate the world model’s ability to learn world rules, we propose the Link-Ablation-Fusion Game (LAFG) benchmark, including some puzzle games as small worlds with well-defined and finite set of world rules. We investigate Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) as the world model to simulate these small game worlds. The LAFG benchmark is challenged and fine-tuned MLLMs struggle with establishing causality between different visual tokens. We propose incorporating various transitional texts to aid the language model in establishing relationships between the different visual world states. Extensive experimental results show the effectiveness of transitional texts. ©2025 IEEE.
