DO  - 10.1109/ICASSP55912.2026.11463569
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1109/ICASSP55912.2026.11463569
AB  - Sample-inefficient in Deep Reinforcement Learning (DRL) impedes scalability and practical deployment. While model-based Reinforcement Learning (MBRL) improves efficiency through environmental simulation, it suffers from model bias and high computational costs. Conversely, conventional optimizers adapt poorly to DRL’s non-stationary optimization landscapes. To bridge this gap, we introduce DynaPredict, a novel optimization framework that transposes the concept of environmental simulation from observation space to parameter space. Instead of predicting observations, DynaPredict employs a lightweight predictive component to forecast the trajectory of policy parameter updates, thereby implicitly encoding environmental feedback. Our method alternates between predictive and real update cycles, drastically reducing environment interactions while avoiding model bias. Empirically, DynaPredict achieves consistent performance gains across the Atari benchmark, reducing sample complexity by up to 60% and accelerating wall-clock convergence time, demonstrating benefits orthogonal to specific algorithms or parallel architectures.
